"A wide-column data model shows query-oriented tables, partition keys, clustering order, and replicated entity representations.\n\nUse when: Design bounded partitions and efficient ordered reads. Review denormalized tables supporting specific queries.\n\nAvoid when: A normalized schema with arbitrary joins is expected.\n\nVisual grammar: Table cards distinguish partition-key columns from ordered clustering columns. Partition examples group rows sharing the complete partition key. Query annotations identify equality filters, range bounds, and required ordering.\n\nBuild: Enumerate access queries and their expected result bounds. Choose partition and clustering columns for each query table. Show representative rows in clustering order without exposing real data. Place query annotations beside table cards with unobstructed key labels.\n\nCheck: The full partition key is clear, including composite components. Clustering order matches the intended range reads. Duplicated data has a stated update strategy. Partition boundaries and row separators leave all key text readable.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming primary-key fields have identical roles. Creating an unbounded partition merely because the query is convenient.\n\nExample: events_by_device_day partitions by device and day, with event time and event ID defining clustering order.\n\nAlternatives: Key-value access model, Storage partition diagram."
